      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5a95256f54d514123c1f089d|full_name}: Unlike bikes and cars these do the same thing, the Index has individual finger tracking but that's barely an improvement on gestures. It's more like getting a car with a touchscreen that can't turn left 1/3 times. Your argument is \"it's more advanced technology\" but that doesn't matter when base functionality wasn't maintained. I would much rather have the old car that still drives reliably, just like having the Oculus controllers is better than the Index controllers because you don't need to constantly deal with Victory or Rock N Roll gestures failing, followed by a repeated calibration process to try to fix them where you drum your fingers along their length.\n\nFrankly it's night and day, the base functionality of doing reliable gestures every time without fail is a tragic loss and Valve failed this generation of VR controllers to push out a half-baked gimmick on controllers with so many hardware faults I can't imagine someone wasn't fired over it.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
